ut politics  it's about reasoning strategy when you  look at the Flat Earth movement in the  United States and you know there's  debate over how much of that movement is  sort of satirical in nature but at least  some significant portion of it is not  satirical there's there's really no way  to deny that right what what do you make  of the growth of that movement well I  don't know if you know this but I went  to the Flat Earth convention in Denver  in November of 2018 yeah and there were  600 Flattr folks there and  and the first day I went incognito I  mean I bought the badge and I went  because I study science and all I wanted  to see what was up and I'm here to tell  you they were serious they were not it  was not satirical there were true  believers of plenty there were seminars  how to recruit new folks for Flat Earth  and it's a it's a growing rising  movement I think within 10 or 15 years  they're going to be Flat Earth there's  running for school board and trying to  get us to quote/unquote teach the  controversy in the public schools same  way that they're now doing for climate  change same way that happened a decade  or more ago with the evolution by  natural selection it's it's a growing  movement but they are absolutely dead  serious about this they think that they  know the truth and the rest of us have  been brainwashed one of the things that  I've experienced myself when talking  either it could be too flat earthers  could be too vaccine deniers it doesn't  really matter who is that you often  start the conversation about the merits  of the particular subject like it's a  conversation about flat earth vs.  spherical earth for example but then at  a certain point it gets into the more  general distrust or myth that comes from  a misunderstanding of what science is  and the scientific method there's often  this idea that listen science has been  wrong at the the the conclusions of  science have been wrong in the past  therefore science as a process is 