're not so  it's sort of like their people are right  to be skeptical of individuals  identified as experts in some ways but  on the other hand it's having this this  very detrimental effect within the field  of science and I don't know how we deal  with that problem it is it is a real  problem because if you look at the way  that scientific issues are covered in  that in the media and I have to say  especially on television they often  engage in false equivalence because it's  so easy to have a split screen they'll  have somebody you know from NASA on one  side somebody with a website on the  other they'll give them equal time and  then at the end say well it's a  controversial issue you decide turning  to the audience that's not the way to  present scientific issues they often the  media because they're looking for an  angle they want to stir controversy  where there isn't one which was  disastrous for ante vats I mean look at  what the trouble that we're having now  and it's because of all the media  attention pretending that there was a  controversy where there's really not I  think the same thing happened with  climate change it's it's really  irresponsible to have that sort of false  equivalence happens all the time in the  political arena  with predictable results when it happens  with science I think you face a real  problem because the I mean the halfway  point between the truth and a lie is  still a lie can we talk a little bit  about what methods you think are  effective to kind of disabuse people of  these false notions either about science  in general or about particular issues if  you want and the way I would sort of  frame it is we know that as a general  kind of behavioral economics cognitive  psychology sort of sort of rule or  understanding that just giving people  contradictory facts to what they believe  usually backfires it often has the  effect of making people just seek more  confirmatory evidence for what they  already believe the measles out