 they'll talk about not in  this particular context but for example  with the challenging dangerous extremist  ideas when is it worth ignoring them  versus when it when is it worth actually  having conversations and and pushing  back and I don't know that in in the  political space there's a  one-size-fits-all answer what I've said  is when my assessment is that the ideas  are prevalent enough that left unchecked  they could grow out of control I want to  make an effort to challenge those ideas  but I don't know how you make that  calculus on the science side it's it's  tough I mean if you're having a values  debate you you probably can't convince  somebody you'd like to think that if it  were an empirical debate that eventually  people will you know that you could make  some progress on something like this and  so I mean it is it is probably more  difficult in the political arena that it  is in the empirical arena but I mean you  do find people in the empirical arena  changing their mind I remember the story  a few years back of the mayor of Coral  Gables Florida who is a staunch  Republican four days into his mayorship  changed his mind on climate change and  is because he saw the reality of what he  was what he was dealing with so it's  it's very hard to change people's mind  but I think it's almost impossible  unless you engage them in debate and try  to control the terms of the debate that  is make it make it about facts or I'll  tell you one question that I used with  great effect at the Flat Earth  convention what evidence would it take  to convince you that you were wrong yes  the diffusability test which I was first  introduced to by by Peter Bogosian  actually but I know is something that's  been around been around for a while very  effective in my ex  is what it stopped him they couldn't  really answer it I'm not sure how much  ground I made on it but it it stopped  him in their tracks because I don't  think they'd ever heard that question  before we've been speaking at least in  part about what's in the great book by  Lee MacIntyre the scientific attitude  defending science from denial fraud and  pseudoscience Lee really a pleasure  having you on thank you so much I really  enjoyed it

